===Etymology 1===
First use appears c. 1370. {{inh|en|enm|clokke}}, {{m|enm|clok}}, {{m|enm|cloke}}, from {{der|en|dum|clocke||bell, clock}}, from {{der|en|odt||*klokka}}, from {{der|en|ML.|clocca}}, probably of {{der|en|cel|-}} origin, from {{der|en|cel-pro|*klokkos||bell}} (compare {{cog|cy|cloch}}, {{cog|sga|cloc}}), either {{onomatopoeic|en|title=onomatopoeic}} or from {{der|en|ine-pro|*klek-||to laugh, cackle}} (compare {{cog|gem-pro|*hlahjaną||to laugh}}). Related to {{cog|ang|clucge}}, {{cog|nl|klok}}, {{cog|stq|Klokke||bell; clock}}, {{cog|nds|Klock||bell, clock}}, {{cog|de|Glocke}}, {{cog|sv|klocka}}. {{doublet|en|cloak|cloche}}.

