====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|Q376}}A [[chronometer]], an [[instrument]] that [[measure]]s [[time]], [[particularly]] the [[time of day]].
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|II||The seasons bring the flower again,<br>⁠And bring the firstling to the flock;<br>⁠And in the dusk of thee, the '''clock'''<br>Beats out the little lives of men.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1980|title=AA Book of British Villages|publisher=Drive Publications Ltd|page=163|text=An interesting feature of the church is the invisible '''clock''', which you can hear thumping away as you enter. Constructed in 1525, it is one of the oldest timepieces in England. It chimes the hours and the quarters, and every three hours it plays a hymn. But it has no faces.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author= Richard Klein |chapter= Introduction |title= Cigarettes are sublime |edition= Paperback |location= Durham |publisher= Duke University Press |year= 1995 |year_published= 1993 |isbn= 0-8223-1641-2 |oclc= 613939086 |page= 8 |pageurl= https://books.google.com/books?id=E0i4xgH4vOgC&pg=PA8&lpg=PA8&dq=clocks |passage=In the June days of 1848 Baudelaire reports seeing revolutionaries (he might have been one of them) going through the streets of Paris with rifles, shooting all the '''clocks'''.}}
# {{lb|en|[[attributive]]}} A common noun relating to an instrument that measures or keeps track of time.
#: {{ux|en|A 12-hour '''clock''' system; an antique '''clock''' sale; Acme is a '''clock''' manufacturer.}}
# {{lb|en|British}} The [[odometer]] of a [[motor vehicle]].
#: {{ux|en|This car has over 300,000 miles on the '''clock'''.}}
# {{lb|en|electronics}} An [[electrical]] [[signal]] that [[synchronize]]s [[timing]] among [[digital]] [[circuit]]s of [[semiconductor]] [[chip]]s or [[module]]s.
# The [[seed]] [[head]] of a [[dandelion]].
# A [[time clock]].
#: {{ux|en|I can't go off to lunch yet: I'm still on the '''clock'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|We let the guys use the shop's tools and equipment for their own projects as long as they're off the '''clock'''.}}
# {{lb|en|computing|informal}} A [[CPU]] clock [[cycle]], or [[T-state]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1984|title=The Journal of Forth Application and Research|volume=2|page=83
|passage=Executing a NEXT to code takes 7 '''clocks''', or 1.05 microseconds.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1990|author=Joseph F. Traub; Barbara J. Grosz|title=Annual Review of Computer Science|page=180
|passage=The best schedule produced by any hardware algorithm takes 7 '''clocks''', whereas the statically reordered code in Figure 1.2(b) takes only 5 '''clocks'''.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A [[luck]]-[[based]] [[patience]] or [[solitaire]] [[card game]] with the [[card]]s [[laid out]] to [[represent]] the [[face]] of a [[clock]].
#: {{syn|en|clock patience}}
# {{lb|en|UK|obsolete|thieves}} A [[watch]] {{gl|timepiece}}.
#* Arthur Morrison, ''Chance of the Game''
#*: But if the '''clock''' was a [[red 'un]], and the opportunity undoubted; to be pinched in the Bow Road merely might well imply loss of caste in the mob, but nobody need be ashamed to be pinched anywhere for a gold watch, after all.

=====Usage notes=====
* ''Clock'' originally denoted a mechanical timekeeping device that was able to mark the time with chimes or another sounding mechanism, distinguished from a {{m|en|timepiece}} which had no such mechanism and a {{m|en|horologe}} and other terms inclusive of [[sundial]]s, [[clepsydra]]s, and similar devices. ''Clock ''is now the general term for all timekeeping devices, inclusive of aspects of software that tracks and displays the time, but as a physical object it is still sometimes distinguished from a small portable {{m|en|watch}} and from nonmechanical timekeeping devices.

